00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:03.918
All right, let's get back to the show.

00:06:03.918 --> 00:06:05.940
(somber music)

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:08.740
Okay, so that could potentially mean that

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:11.040
one, obviously, the pulmonary
embolism we just talked about,

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.580
but also a lot of stress on her heart.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:14.560
Something could have happened there,

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:15.870
congestive heart failure-wise,

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.450
but also patients that are of her size

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:20.680
do develop sleep apnea
where they stop breathing

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:21.520
for periods of time,

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:23.210
but usually it's not to the point

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:25.040
where their heart stops as well.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:28.120
- She put her body to limit for years

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:34.100
and the car ride down was just
too much for her to handle.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:36.170
We need to get her to surgery immediately

00:06:36.170 --> 00:06:37.870
to put the filter in her.

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.160
- So what he's talking
about here is an IVC filter,

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:42.050
which is an inferior vena cava filter,

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:44.240
which is meant to be put into the vein

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:46.560
in order to catch and act like a net

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.980
for clots that could
potentially leave the lower body

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:51.250
and come up to the lungs,

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:52.840
so prevents them from reaching the lungs.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:56.630
Now, usually we do this
only as a secondary resort,

00:06:56.630 --> 00:06:59.170
because in patients who have blood clots,

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:01.520
we usually put them on
blood thinning agents

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:04.170
unless there is a true contraindication

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:05.750
for starting the blood thinners,

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:07.530
or that they scan her legs

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:08.900
and they see so many clots there

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:10.880
that they don't even think
that they can dissolve them.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:11.713
In this case,

00:07:11.713 --> 00:07:12.970
I'm not sure why they decided

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:15.530
to go with the IVC filter
instead of blood thinning agents,

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:17.870
but they could be doing
both at the same time.

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.770
That's also been done in the past as well.

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:23.880
- And I'm currently placing
the filter in the vena cava.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:25.580
This is a delicate operation

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:28.700
so we have to do this very carefully,

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:31.000
but it will help save Marla's life.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:32.130
- It's important to point out,

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:34.360
'cause I don't think Dr. Now
is making this very clear.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:36.380
This isn't the treatment to save her life,

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:40.660
this is a treatment to prevent
another clot from happening

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:42.560
and dislodging and going to the lungs,

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:44.620
and also it's not 100% effective.

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:47.120
(somber music)

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:48.960
That's a very aggressive diet.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.700
I don't know if she needed
that aggressive of a diet

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:52.640
right off the bat.

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:55.670
Even eating 2,000 calories a
day would cause weight loss.

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:58.180
I don't think you needed
it to be so extreme.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:01.053
- Outside of having to
adjust to a controlled diet,

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:04.513
they've been trying to get me up to walk.

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:07.330
I feel like they're pushing me too hard.

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:09.210
- So one of the orders
that we almost always

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:10.930
put in for patients that are hospitalized

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:14.280
for extended period of time
is ambulate with assistance.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:17.370
Ambulate at least from
out of bed to chair,

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:18.940
because that allows the patient

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:21.030
to decrease the likelihood
of developing clots.

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:22.870
It helps facilitate the recovery,

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:25.170
starts working the cardiovascular system.

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:27.160
Because a patient who's so deconditioned,

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:28.900
meaning not used to moving around,

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:32.150
needs to gradually work their
way up in order to return

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:34.615
to be able to have some
level of independence.

00:08:34.615 --> 00:08:37.615
(suspenseful music)

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:41.230
- Hi.
- Hi.

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:43.410
- When someone become immobile,

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:46.420
they get used to others
doing everything for them.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:50.600
That can be a tough dynamic to change.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:51.570
- You ready?
- Yeah.

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:53.520
- Okay, I'm gonna bring this one first.

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.420
- [Dr. Nowzaradan] But we are
gonna do what we can to try.

00:08:57.375 --> 00:08:58.770
- Are you ready, al right.

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:01.210
- The rest will be up to Marla.

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:02.208
- Push.

00:09:02.208 --> 00:09:04.671
(Marla groans)

00:09:04.671 --> 00:09:06.830
- I'm falling, please!
- It's okay, we got you.

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:10.110
- If Marla won't get up
then we can't treat her.

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:12.840
- Yeah, being able to move
and carry your own body weight

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.770
is very important in order for her

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:17.030
to have success of any surgery,

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:18.490
'cause, again, you need to have your heart

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:19.970
strong enough to be able to do that.

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:22.620
Part of my risk stratification
for preoperative screening

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:25.260
is finding out what a patient's
activity levels are like.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:26.270
What are they able to do?

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:28.440
For example, if a patient
actively exercises,

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.070
they're automatically a lower risk

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:32.440
versus a patient who's
sedentary, doesn't exercise,

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:34.400
gets winded just by walking a block.

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:36.250
That patient automatically
becomes higher risk.

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:37.150
When I say higher risk,

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:39.060
I mean higher risk for
their heart stopping,

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:41.146
like that's the risk I'm talking about.

00:09:41.146 --> 00:09:42.370
(suspenseful music)

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:45.920
- I'm going to go ahead and
perform weight loss surgery

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:48.480
because outside of this, she has no hope.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:51.750
Gastric bypass surgery
reduce size of stomach

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:54.013
and also bypass part of the intestine.

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:58.163
The stomach is big as a watermelon,

00:09:58.163 --> 00:10:01.050
but this operation, we're
gonna reduce size of stomach

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:02.910
to the size of a small orange.

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:05.470
- This sounds and looks
barbaric because it is,

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:06.890
but it's an extreme measure

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:09.460
to potentially save a person's life.

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:11.940
This is why we don't
recommend bariatric surgery

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:14.330
from someone who's only
somewhat overweight.

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:16.490
Every treatment has risks.

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:19.310
In fact, if a treatment
doesn't have risks,

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:20.570
it likely, in the beginning

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:22.800
never had any potential benefit anyway.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:26.590
- Marla is demonstrating
very poor attitude

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:28.160
and very poor response.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.650
This is a very dysfunctional behavior.

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:33.260
She stumble like that,

00:10:33.260 --> 00:10:35.710
if she act like a kid,
you treated like a kid.

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:38.350
- It's a very controversial
way to be a physician.

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:40.130
I find that I've had success

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:41.810
with other motivational techniques,

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:44.080
making a healthier
connection with your patient,

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:45.920
using motivational
interviewing techniques.

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:47.440
Granted it doesn't work all the time.

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:49.070
Patient needs to know there's consequences

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:49.903
for their actions.

00:10:49.903 --> 00:10:50.920
You could tell the Dr. Now

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:52.050
is getting a little bit offended,

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:53.180
getting a little irritated.

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:55.760
Pulling yourself outta that
situation is probably ideal

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:57.960
because now, while you're
using this old school

00:10:57.960 --> 00:10:59.970
like hardened type of mentality,

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:01.180
doesn't work for all people

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:03.470
and can actually have the
patient turn against you.

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:05.510
- [Dr. Nowzaradan] I'm
hoping that sending her home

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:06.873
will be a wake up call.

00:11:08.306 --> 00:11:11.020
(somber music)

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:13.060
- But I wonder why they
need Dr. Now for this.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:16.040
Dr. Now is not a
rehabilitation specialist.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:18.070
Maybe getting her in with
a mental health specialist

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:19.590
would've probably been
the best thing here.

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:22.550
And while Dr. Now could be
a great bariatric surgeon,

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:24.390
he's clearly not a
mental health specialist.

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:26.700
He's clearly not a rehab specialist,

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:29.020
and while all of his advice
medically makes sense,

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:30.290
he's clearly not being effective

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:31.440
in communicating that message

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:33.910
and getting the patient to
take care of her best interest.

00:11:33.910 --> 00:11:35.270
Getting another team member here

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:37.210
would go a long way in helping her

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:40.947
perhaps find her inner
motivation to wanna stand up.

00:11:40.947 --> 00:11:42.347
(upbeat music)

00:11:42.347 --> 00:11:46.790
- Here we go, all right.

00:11:46.790 --> 00:11:48.267
Keep pushing your weight off.

00:11:48.267 --> 00:11:50.371
(Marla groans)

00:11:50.371 --> 00:11:52.090
- All right.
- Okay.

00:11:52.090 --> 00:11:53.320
- Good job, Marla.

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:56.177
- Yeah, I didn't think I could get up.

00:11:56.177 --> 00:11:58.280
- You wanna try it again?

00:11:58.280 --> 00:11:59.113
- Yeah, we can.

00:11:59.113 --> 00:12:01.650
- This is the time and tested true method,

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:04.410
time tested true, true time tested method

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:06.670
of motivation doesn't come first.

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:09.660
It's doing the action and
then the motivation follows,

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:11.240
like her saying, "I wanna do it again."

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:12.470
- There you go.
- Keep going, keep going.

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:13.690
- Keep going, you're almost there.

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:14.850
- Keep going, you're almost there.

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:17.140
- [Nurse] All right.
- [Doctor] There you go.

00:12:17.140 --> 00:12:19.143
- Wow, wow, that's great weight loss.

00:12:19.143 --> 00:12:19.976
(nurses cheering)

00:12:19.976 --> 00:12:21.478
- [Nurse] Good job.

00:12:21.478 --> 00:12:23.290
- I'm so proud of myself.

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:25.160
Makes me feel like I can do anything.

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:27.180
- But 300 pound less, huh?

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:28.910
Can you tell the difference?

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:29.743
- Yeah.

00:12:29.743 --> 00:12:31.680
- Breathing is probably easier for her.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:33.530
Because your lungs cannot fully expand

00:12:33.530 --> 00:12:35.390
against all of that excess skin, weight,

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:38.380
and as a result, parts of
your lungs actually die off

00:12:38.380 --> 00:12:40.190
through a process called atelectasis.

00:12:40.190 --> 00:12:42.100
- So when can I get seen in surgery?

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:43.380
- From what I can tell,

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:45.590
you're not making much
effort to get better.

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:46.910
- That's your opinion.

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:50.670
I don't think I have a very
good understanding with you

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:51.880
and you don't have one with me.

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:53.450
- I think that's a very fair assessment.

00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:55.830
He's jumping to conclusions
it seems like all the time.

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:59.170
As a doctor you need to
be doing this as a team,

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:00.640
like a collaborative approach.

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:01.910
Not just with you and the patient,

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:03.580
but you, the patient,
the physical therapist,

00:13:03.580 --> 00:13:04.660
you have to be getting the reports.

00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:06.847
If the reports were coming in
from the physical therapist,

00:13:06.847 --> 00:13:08.560
"That patient's not
interested in doing this,"

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:09.393
that's one thing.

00:13:09.393 --> 00:13:11.100
But my suspicion is
he's making assumptions

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:13.380
based on her weight or lack of progress.

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:15.500
Now, you need to be investigating
this lack of progress,

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:16.573
where it's coming from.

00:13:16.573 --> 00:13:17.580
You can't just make assumptions.

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:20.610
- In the 5,000 patient in past 40 years,

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:23.640
I don't remember I've seen
anybody six months after surgery

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:25.770
that hadn't been able to walk.

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:26.902
- You're putting me group.

00:13:26.902 --> 00:13:29.027
- My opinion is based on my experience.

00:13:29.027 --> 00:13:31.270
- You can't put me with nobody else.

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:33.280
- You got the bad attitude.

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:34.830
- And why do I have a bad attitude?

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:38.130
- Time out, Dr. Now, separate yourself.

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:41.340
You can have this same exact
conversation, same theme,

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:43.240
same message that he's
trying to get across

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:45.230
without ever getting mad.

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:47.640
You are literally doing the opposite

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:49.460
of de-escalating the situation.

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:51.670
- I feel you don't treat me fair or right.

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:53.770
- Marla, I can't do anymore for you.

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:54.603
- Okay.

00:13:54.603 --> 00:13:55.436
- See your primary doctor,

00:13:55.436 --> 00:13:57.640
ask him tomorrow to find another surgeon

00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:00.120
because we are dismissing
you from our care.

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:01.450
- The patient makes a statement.

00:14:01.450 --> 00:14:03.680
- I feel you don't treat me fair or right.

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:04.513
- [Dr. Now.] Marla.

00:14:04.513 --> 00:14:05.710
- You need to explore that as a physician.

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:06.760
What do you mean by that?

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:09.370
Patient will say, "I
want this skin surgery

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:10.710
and you're not giving this to me."

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:11.847
I can then say to the patient,

00:14:11.847 --> 00:14:13.860
"I would love to give
you the skin surgery,

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:16.480
but I know that if we do it
before you start walking,

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:17.920
ultimately, it's gonna harm you.

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:20.990
My goal here is to treat
you not to treat your skin.

00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:23.530
It's to make you as healthy
as a person as possible

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:25.470
and not put you into negative situations

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:26.600
or dangerous situation.

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:28.610
Isn't that what you want
for yourself as well?"

00:14:28.610 --> 00:14:30.640
You see how we just all
got on the same page?

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:31.920
That might not work the first time.

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:33.410
That might have to take multiple things,

00:14:33.410 --> 00:14:34.580
but that's where you gotta be patient.

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:36.170
You gotta be patient with your patients.

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:38.010
- I finally found the
clinic here in Nashville

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:41.080
to help me keep making
progress with my weight loss.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:43.220
I hope I made it below 400 pounds.

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:44.800
- Wow.

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:45.770
- 364.

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:46.830
- And she's standing.

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:48.450
- Ms. McCants.
- How you doing?

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:49.830
- Nice to see you.

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:52.610
Well, you have made a standing progress,

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:54.710
and if you can lose that next 100 pounds

00:14:54.710 --> 00:14:57.480
then you can have all
the extra skin taken off.

00:14:57.480 --> 00:14:58.313
- Yes.

00:14:58.313 --> 00:15:00.170
- If you do the skin
surgery also too early,

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:01.880
you then still lose more weight.

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:03.210
You'll need more surgery,

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:05.280
means more scars, more complications.

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:07.700
You also have to be understanding
that having excess skin

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:10.450
makes it challenging to
mobilize and move around.

00:15:10.450 --> 00:15:12.037
Be understanding and say,

00:15:12.037 --> 00:15:13.740
"I understand that this is terrible,

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:15.670
but we're gonna have to
keep moving through this.

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:16.900
So what ways can I help you?"

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:18.910
Maybe you can get them
outfit recommendations,

00:15:18.910 --> 00:15:21.510
compression stockings, whatever it is

00:15:21.510 --> 00:15:24.210
to try and help your
patient achieve their goals.

00:15:24.210 --> 00:15:25.820
- [Marla] Once I get this skin off of me

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:27.950
and nothing's holding me back anymore,

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:29.920
I'm gonna travel and see the world.

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:31.580
- Genuinely, her life has changed.

00:15:31.580 --> 00:15:33.960
Her opportunities have expanded greatly.

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:35.270
And I'm not talking about

00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:37.230
her opportunities to travel
the world, that's cool,

00:15:37.230 --> 00:15:39.590
but her ability to move to the kitchen,

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:41.630
her ability to prepare food for herself.

00:15:41.630 --> 00:15:42.870
That if one of her kids get sick,

00:15:42.870 --> 00:15:45.090
she could still care for her daily needs.

00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:47.030
Imagine that if one of
her kids can't come,

00:15:47.030 --> 00:15:48.840
she would have to not be able to shower

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:51.110
and potentially get infections
then get hospitalized.

00:15:51.110 --> 00:15:53.080
It's not a quality of
life that is reasonable.

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:56.600
So that's why bariatric surgery
absolutely needs to exist,

00:15:56.600 --> 00:15:57.433
at least for now.

00:15:57.433 --> 00:15:59.280
We need to be open-minded to its risks

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:01.830
and be aware of its risks and
not overuse it as a treatment.

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:03.640
- We can't wait to show
you how she looks now.

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:05.561
Please welcome, Miss Marla McCants.

00:16:05.561 --> 00:16:08.202
(women cheering)

00:16:08.202 --> 00:16:09.330
- Wow.

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:10.200
- You're not alone.

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:13.300
You can overcome your struggles and fears.

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:14.250
You can overcome.

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:16.490
You can do it, I did it, you can do it.

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:17.760
- Post traumatic growth right there.

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:20.210
Someone went through a terrible situation,

00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:21.900
both, initially, with her partner,

00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:24.480
but now also a traumatic
situation with her weight,

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:26.240
and now she's a role model for people.

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:29.840
She's using that trauma to
grow from to help others grow.

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:32.170
Yay, I'm team Marla.

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:33.410
Actually, not too long ago,

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:35.790
I was accused of being
fat phobic on Reddit.

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:37.000
Click here for that video

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:38.820
where I sort of explained my stance.

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:40.370
I think it's a worthwhile watch,

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:42.030
and again, huge thank you to ZipRecruiter

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:43.370
for sponsoring this video.

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:45.395
As always stay happy and healthy.

00:16:45.395 --> 00:16:47.978
(upbeat music)

